# `_KDB` namespaces and state

## What goes where

| Namespace | Holds |
|---|---|
| `_KDB.state.<subtree>` | JSON-serialisable dashboard/session state: selections, confirmed settings, active tab, visibility/presence status, load lifecycle, history stack |
| `_KDB.runtime.<owner>` | technical, non-serialisable resources (see `runtime.mdc`) |
| `_KDB.api.<module>` / `_KDB.services.<service>` | public APIs (see `module-public-api.mdc`) |
| `_KDB.events` | the event bus and nothing else |
| `_KDB.config` | declarative project config, labels, option lists (`_KDB.config.options`, built in `core/config`) |
| `_KDB.properties` | values read from the Cognos property panel (`spec.json`); read-only, not state |
| `_KDB.elements.selectors.<module>` | selector strings only (see `dom.mdc`) |
| `_KDB.utils` | pure helpers published by `core/utils` (the kit in `core/utils/kit/` is imported via AMD instead) |
| `_KDB.cognosAPI` | inner Cognos ring only (see `cognos-adapter.mdc`) |
| `_KDB.data` | reserved, unused |

- No other top-level keys (`_KDB.cache`, `_KDB.tmp`, ...). `_KDB.dropdownOptions`, `_KDB.utilities` and `_KDB.generalFunctions` do not exist; do not reintroduce them. Options live in `_KDB.config.options`.
- Do not use `_KDB` to avoid an AMD import of code. Do not replace a whole namespace from feature code. Never serialise `_KDB` as a whole.

`_KDB.state` never holds DOM or jQuery objects, listeners, AbortControllers, timers, observers, promises, Cognos objects, functions, pointer/hover/drag mechanics, option lists or property values.

## One canonical writer per subtree

| Subtree | Writer |
|---|---|
| `state.header`, `footer`, `tabs`, `launchpad`, `kdbControl`, `dragDrop`, `metricsTree`, `authoring` | the module of the same name |
| `state.widgets.<sub>` (`base`, `settings`, `vis`, `text`, `topN`, `dataTable`) | the matching widget module |
| `state.filters.<sub>` | the filter owner module (singleSelect, dienststelle, sicht, ...) |
| `state.tableData`, `state.history`, `state.loading` | the service of the same name |
| `state.rootLifecycle` | `KDB.js` |

The full map is `architecture/stateOwnership.md`. Any other code reads through the owner's selectors and changes the subtree only by `_KDB.api.<owner>.dispatch(...)` or a service method. This applies to event subscribers, Cognos event bridges and the writeback restore as well: no "just one field" foreign writes.

```js
_KDB.state.launchpad.items.push(widget);                                                   // forbidden
_KDB.api.launchpad.dispatch({ type: "launchpad/sendWidget", payload: { widgetId: id } });  // correct
```

Inside the owner, reducers (`state.js`) produce the next state. Selectors are plain functions `readX(_KDB, ...)`; they return the stored value or a cheap derived value and do not deep-clone the whole subtree per call.

## Lifecycle and freshness

- `lifecycle` is a string from `.cursor/lifecycleVocabulary.md`, never `null` or a boolean. The owner lists the terms it uses in a header comment of `index.js`.
- `freshness` (`fresh` / `stale`) is a separate field. No hybrid terms like `stale-error`.
- Error payload fields: `errorCode`, `errorMessage` (see `error-handling.mdc`).

## Cognos mirror

Some values exist both in `_KDB.state` and in Cognos (filter selections, active tab, widget visualisation). `_KDB.state` is authoritative for our logic, Cognos for its engine. The owner that writes the mirrored value also performs the matching cognosHost facet call. After Cognos-driven changes the owner re-syncs; it does not assume every change came through our code. Mirror a multi-step Cognos update only once it is complete, never its intermediate states (see `cognos-adapter.mdc`).

## Ids

Ids used by restore or writeback (widget ids, tab ids) stay stable across save and restore. Tab-bound state carries the tab id, not only a position. Rebuild state on restore before handlers read it.

## New state and unclear ownership

A new subtree needs: a named owner, an initial shape, selectors, and an entry in `architecture/stateOwnership.md`.

When two modules look like natural writers: pick the one that initialises the value and owns its lifecycle; the other calls its API. If none clearly wins, check whether it is really two different values and split them (for example Cognos' active tab mirror versus our restore target tab). Do not create a "neutral" router service just to own contested state.
